ver at corporate av  where gabe once worked how big of a  change have you seen year over year  pre-pandemic to right now we might be 25  percent  of what we would have been normally in  this time  owner joe gilderson says the pandemic  has shrunk his live event business  into a series of less profitable virtual  gatherings  some of which may become permanent do  you think  the in-person event business is going to  come back like it was  i don't think every event will come back  to being  hundreds of people together i think  certain events will probably stay online  it means that you can't fully heal from  this not quickly  and that means former employees like mo  johnson can't heal quickly either  i did have to ask for some extensions  here and there  we borrow for my 401k and a year without  full-time work  has meant a year without medical  coverage something we first discussed  last summer  my wife and i actually had a spat today  she said you know you should go to the  dentist and i'm like we don't have  insurance  lately he made another medical  compromise putting off the purchase of  new contact lenses  honestly i do need some more but i've  been really washing  cleaning and oh man but that's that's  dangerous man i know  disposable ones yeah knock on wood you  don't have an infection  yeah you gotta clean those things it's  only thanks to unemployment benefits and  federal stimulus money that former  full-time employees like mo johnson have  been able to keep up with their bills  this is a cushion to make your life a  little softer between now and when  things pick back  up well i i i don't call it a cushion  it's  it's hard earned i've paid into the  system all my life  you know this is what i pay in for  you're not giving me back my money  you're giving me back my investment when  we spoke a year ago  gabe's wife claudia feared that the  federal government wouldn't be there  when her family needed the help you're  just constantly worried about  like can i make this payment can i put  my kid  you know can i get a doctor even before  even before  but these days even after unexpected  expenses like a damaged fence and broken  appliances  she has a different outlook i don't feel  as skeptical  as i did last year because last year i  just really felt like wow we're just  going to be in an island you know  but this year i feel like okay i think  the government  is hearing us and trying to get  somewhere  how important has the coveted stimulus  been to keeping you guys afloat this  year  it's the only reason we're afloat one  full year into this pandemic  that's pretty much the goal for families  like the annelles  pay the rent or mortgage make at least  the minimum payments on your credit  cards  you may only be treading water but at  least you're not  going under we're not going to get out  of debt  at all but we can just you know ride the  waves  ride the waves for a little while longer  and that's the best most of us can hope  for  we do want to point out some positive  developments for the people in our story  both gabe and now  and mo johnson have recently been called  in for part-time work at corporate av  that's that audio company they're part  of  and when we were there last week owner  joe gilderson received his very first  two phone calls for live events  being scheduled for the fall these were  ones that had been canceled in 2020.  however he does predict it will be a  slow ramp up and it's likely that not  everyone will be hired back  all at once he had to lay off more than  a dozen people yeah  so moved by gabe's vulnerability yes so  from  feeling demoralized to feeling grateful  and just trying to hold on and  ride the wave and mo saying you know i  built i put money in  that's not a cushion i put out a handout  yeah that's his investment it's his part  of the american drill and the difference  that  that money has made for people it's  really literally as you say just it's  kept them above water  absolutely i like both of them thank you  tony i like them too

